ies to retail customers. The company serves a diverse customer base, including price-driven consumers, style leaders, wellness shoppers with vision benefits, managed care customers, outside prescription customers, and progressive lens wearers. The company was formerly known as National Vision, Inc. and changed its name to National Vision Holdings, Inc. in March 2014. The company was founded in 1990 and is headquartered in Duluth, Georgia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Alexander N. Wilkes | CEO & Director | Age 46 | Compensation: $2.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 13K people. Revenue per employee of $151K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers. Each employee generates $6K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 13\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["National Vision Holdings, Inc. is a small-cap consumer cyclical business with a $1.4B market cap on $2.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -0% over the past three years \u2014 essentially flat, and a business treading water is rarely a path to compounding.","Returns on capital run at 4% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $73M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 4.94\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 3.1\u00d7.","Shareholder yield is only 0.2%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 2.7% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.4\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."